answer:
In a single-process, multi-graph scenario, DMalloc returns the error code 16847020 (HIAI_GRAPH_MEMORY_POOL_STOPPED) because the memory pool is stopped after the first graph is destroyed. This occurs because graphs within the same process share a single memory pool. When the first graph is destroyed, its memory pool is also stopped. The second graph then attempts to allocate memory from a stopped pool, leading to the DMalloc failure with the error code HIAI_GRAPH_MEMORY_POOL_STOPPED.

To handle this issue, it is advised not to destroy the first graph if an exception occurs. Instead, a retry mechanism can be implemented for the error code HIAI_GRAPH_MEMORY_POOL_STOPPED. For example, the following code snippet can be used:

```cpp
int cnt = 0;
while (cnt < 5) {
    if (HIAI_GRAPH_MEMORY_POOL_STOPPED == HIAI_DMalloc(dataSize, timeOut, flag)) {
        usleep(10000);  // Sleep for 10 milliseconds
        cnt++;
    } else {
        break;
    }
}
```

This retry mechanism allows the application to attempt memory allocation again after a brief pause, which can help in cases where the memory pool might be temporarily unavailable.
